Are you familiar with the international phonetic alphabet, IPA?  That
would solve our problems here but we can't use them very well on the
Usenet, so ASCII-IPA is often used instead.  Unfortunately, there are
several schemes, but the Kirshenbaum scheme seems to be a common one.

>> I think it's just the level of transcription Webster provides,
>> differentiating phonemes (schwa in this case) but not always
>> their varying pronunciations (which don't alter meaning).
>
> Schwa?

Yes, a schwa is the sound of an unstressed German <e> (it's customary to
put written words into <>s), like in <Zaehne> or <Ecke>. It's also the
unstressed first sound of English <about> and <abut>.  (The ASCII-IPA
symbol is [@], and the IPA symbol is a "turned e".)  Now, we must make a
difference between American and British English: AmEn has that sound in
the stressed syllable of <abut> as well but (sic!) BrEn/RP has something
different, a sound written [V] in ASCII-IPA ("turned v" in IPA).  I am
not sure of its exact pronunciation, by the way. :> The IPA chart of
vowels seems to place it as the unrounded "version" of [O] (IPA "open
o", "turned c"), which is the vowel of <raw>, at least in BrEn/RP.

_The Origins and Development of the English Language_ by Pyles and Algeo
tells us that <blood> is one of the words where the earlier Middle
English long, closed o, became [u:] (long), eg. <root>, and in some
words laxing to [U] (shorter) happened, in eg. <book>, <foot> and
<good>.  Still in <blood> and <flood> a further unrounding has
occurred. 

If that sounds like Chinese to you, you should observe yourself which
vowels are pronounced with rounded lips and which vowels with unrounded
lips...  Starting with German, <o>, <oe>, <u> and <ue> would be rounded
vowels.  The remaining ones are then unrounded, exept that the schwa
might be somewhere in between.

